def get_slide_data() -> dict:
    return {
        'title': 'Execution 1 — Boundary Conditions',
        'content': '\n## Boundary Conditions Overview\n\nBefore analyzing the execution results, it is crucial to understand the **Boundary Conditions (BCs)** applied in both the fluid (SPARTA DSMC) and solid (OpenFOAM) domains. These define how the spacecraft interacts with the environment and how heat is transferred.\n\n---\n\n### Visualization of Simulation Domains\n\nThe following diagram illustrates the boundary conditions for the **Sequential Hybrid Pipeline**:\n\n<div style="text-align:center; margin: 1.2rem 0;">\n  <img src="../../figures/Result/boundary_conditions.jpeg"\n       style="max-width:900px; width:100%; border-radius:10px;\n              border:1px solid rgba(76,201,240,0.3);\n              box-shadow: 0 6px 24px rgba(0,0,0,0.6);" />\n  <p style="color:#8b949e; font-size:0.78rem; margin-top:0.5rem;">\n    Figure: Left - SPARTA DSMC Axisymmetric Gas Domain. Right - OpenFOAM Solid Thermal Domain.\n  </p>\n</div>\n\n---\n\n### 1. SPARTA DSMC (Gas Domain)\n\nSPARTA operates in a **2D Axisymmetric** domain (cylindrical coordinates $x, y$ where $y \\ge 0$).\n\n| Boundary | Type | SPARTA Syntax | Physical Meaning |\n|:---|:---|:---:|:---|\n| **Inflow (Left)** | Outflow + Emitter | `o` + `emit/face` | Atmosphere enters at $v_\\infty = 2700$ m/s, $T_\\infty = 250$ K. |\n| **Outflow (Right/Top)** | Vacuum / Outflow | `o` | Particles exit the domain freely, representing infinite space. |\n| **Centreline (Bottom)** | Axisymmetric | `a` | Symmetry axis ($y=0$). Particles reflecting here cross the axis. |\n| **Z-dimension** | Periodic | `p` | Required by SPARTA for 2D axisymmetric solver consistency. |\n| **HIAD Surface** | Diffuse Isothermal | `surf_collide diffuse` | Spacecraft surface. Particles reflect diffusely, accommodating to $T_{wall} \\approx 1453$ K. Heat flux $\\dot{q}_{stag}$ is computed here. |\n\n---\n\n### 2. OpenFOAM (Solid Thermal Domain)\n\nOnce SPARTA computes $\\dot{q}_{stag}$, OpenFOAM (`laplacianFoam`) propagates this heat through the solid F-TPS multi-layer stack.\n\n| Boundary | Type | OpenFOAM Syntax | Physical Meaning |\n|:---|:---|:---:|:---|\n| **Outer Surface** | Heat Flux (Neumann) | `fixedGradient` | Heat enters the shield: $\\nabla T = \\dot{q}_{stag} / k_{eff}$. |\n| **Inner Backface** | Adiabatic | `zeroGradient` | Worst-case assumption: no heat escapes the back. Allows measuring maximum payload soak temperature. |\n| **Edges** | Adiabatic | `zeroGradient` | No lateral heat loss at the edges of the simulation box. |\n\nThe coupling is strictly **one-way sequential**: SPARTA aerodynamic heating drives OpenFOAM solid conduction. The solid temperature does not feed back into the aerodynamic solver, which is a standard assumption for fast hypersonic surrogate training.\n'
    }
